	<strings name="main_menu/settings_select/player_setup/player_profile_edit/video_settings/advanced/help_strings">
		<string text=""/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="Full draws the game at your screen's resolution. Xbox draws it at the Xbox's 640x480 and scales it up: softer, and very light on the graphics card."/>
		<string text="Smooths jagged edges. FXAA and SMAA cost little; SSAA and MSAA are sharper and cost more of the graphics card."/>
		<string text="Smooths jagged edges. FXAA and SMAA cost little; MSAA is sharper and costs more of the graphics card."/>
		<string text="Keeps floors and walls sharp into the distance, at a small cost. Off looks as the Xbox did: surfaces blur a few steps away."/>
		<string text="How detailed characters and vehicles stay in the distance. Xbox simplifies them as on a TV; Full keeps their detail for your screen; Highest always draws the most. Auto is Full in VR."/>
		<string text="Draws movement smoothly between the game's 30 updates a second, so that it looks fluid at any frame rate. Off shows each update as it is."/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="Lights characters and weapons smoothly, without the flat facets of the Xbox's lighting, at a small cost."/>
		<string text="Enhanced: plasma bolts, muzzle flashes and blasts light their surroundings smoothly, with a glow. Classic lights them as the Xbox did."/>
		<string text="Grounds characters, vehicles and scenery with soft shade where they meet the world and in their creases. The levels keep their own lighting. Not yet on Mac."/>
		<string text="Shields light their surroundings as they flare, in the shield's own color. Online: the host's choice."/>
		<string text="Light Show: the shields' glow reaches three times as far, and is brighter. Online: the host's choice."/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="How often the world updates each second, in games on this device: the campaign, split screen and bots. 60 and 120 respond faster and take more of the processor. Online games play at their host's tick rate: an OpenH1 game's (OpenH1 60 Tick), a Legacy game's 30."/>
	</strings>
